te citizens of Tulsa bombed  and burned down the Greenwood  neighborhood will wipes out the Cyclops  his base of operations and he takes one  of their projectors with him it's an eye  for an eye type of justice that makes as  much sense as something like that can  given the circumstances  later he uses the same technology to get  chief Crawford to hang himself while  Crawford does have ties to the Cyclops  at least in his family this situation  feels a lot more like vengeance than  justice will does confront him about the  Klan robes in his closet he doesn't have  a great comeback other than they're a  part of his legacy but he is hypnotized  and because of that we don't get a  better explanation but rather a  statement that will doesn't know what's  really going on here that opens up the  door to there being more to the story  that wills overlooking it definitely  leaves you wondering if will has made a  mistake and that the conspiracy here  might be deeper than what we've seen in  the end that makes it a very fascinating  episode of television of course that  looked great the way they put it  together was great it's good as a  standalone but as far as the whole story  it reinforces this idea that there's  rarely black and white answers to  questions of good and evil in the  Supplemental materials we learned that  while Angela was experiencing all of  this she's also talking and Laurie was  there recording at all  that's how agent Petey knew about the  hooded justice reveal and when I think  was one of the key scenes we'll freaks  out after he sees his son trying to  dress up in his costume as he's trying  to wash the makeup off his face his wife  makes her  intentions to leave and go back to Tulsa  clear she says that she thought that  wearing the mask would help him get rid  of his anger but it didn't get rid of it  it just fed it that seems to be tied  into what this series is trying to say  it's still a little bit early to figure  out how they're going to get t